## Description

The reverse-mode gradient of `categorical_lpmf` for an array of outcomes is NaN whenever the probability vector has an entry that is exactly zero, even when no outcome selects that entry. The log density itself is finite and correct, and the scalar overload gives the correct gradient for the same data. `multinomial_lpmf` does the same for a zero count at a zero probability.

Exact zeros appear in practice through underflow, for example in a softmax with one very negative logit. The NaN then reaches every parameter the simplex depends on.

The cause is a term with zero weight whose reverse pass divides zero by zero:

- The array overload of `categorical_lpmf` takes `log(theta)` of every entry and then sums the entries that the outcomes select ([categorical_lpmf.hpp#L49-L57](https://github.com/stan-dev/math/blob/3d16e8f14e620e2cd35cc59bf92766ad98a9559b/stan/math/prim/prob/categorical_lpmf.hpp#L49-L57)). An entry that no outcome selects still leaves a `log` node on the tape. Its adjoint is 0, and the reverse pass of `log` adds `adj / val` ([rev/fun/log.hpp#L52-L55](https://github.com/stan-dev/math/blob/3d16e8f14e620e2cd35cc59bf92766ad98a9559b/stan/math/rev/fun/log.hpp#L52-L55)), which is `0 / 0` when the entry is exactly zero.
- `multinomial_lpmf` adds `lmultiply(ns[i], theta[i])` for every category ([multinomial_lpmf.hpp#L38](https://github.com/stan-dev/math/blob/3d16e8f14e620e2cd35cc59bf92766ad98a9559b/stan/math/prim/prob/multinomial_lpmf.hpp#L38)). For a zero count at a zero probability the value is 0, but the reverse pass computes `adj * 0 / 0` ([rev/fun/lmultiply.hpp#L38](https://github.com/stan-dev/math/blob/3d16e8f14e620e2cd35cc59bf92766ad98a9559b/stan/math/rev/fun/lmultiply.hpp#L38)).

The scalar overload of `categorical_lpmf` reads only the selected entry, and `multinomial_logit_lpmf` already skips zero counts.

## Example

```cpp
#include <stan/math.hpp>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

int main() {
  using stan::math::var;
  using vector_v = Eigen::Matrix<var, Eigen::Dynamic, 1>;

  {  // Array overload: category 1 has probability 0 and is never observed.
    vector_v theta(3);
    theta << 0.0, 0.6, 0.4;
    std::vector<int> y{2, 3, 2};
    var lp = stan::math::categorical_lpmf(y, theta);
    lp.grad();
    std::cout << "categorical, array:   lp = " << lp.val() << "  grad = "
              << theta.adj().transpose() << "\n";
    stan::math::recover_memory();
  }
  {  // The same outcomes, one scalar call each.
    vector_v theta(3);
    theta << 0.0, 0.6, 0.4;
    var lp = 0;
    for (int y : {2, 3, 2}) {
      lp += stan::math::categorical_lpmf(y, theta);
    }
    lp.grad();
    std::cout << "categorical, scalars: lp = " << lp.val() << "  grad = "
              << theta.adj().transpose() << "\n";
    stan::math::recover_memory();
  }
  {  // Multinomial: a zero count at a zero probability.
    vector_v theta(3);
    theta << 0.0, 0.6, 0.4;
    std::vector<int> counts{0, 2, 3};
    var lp = stan::math::multinomial_lpmf(counts, theta);
    lp.grad();
    std::cout << "multinomial:          lp = " << lp.val() << "  grad = "
              << theta.adj().transpose() << "\n";
    stan::math::recover_memory();
  }
  {  // The zero depends on a parameter s; d lp / d s through the array call.
    var s = 0.3;
    var a = stan::math::exp(s - 2000);  // underflows to exactly 0
    var p = stan::math::inv_logit(s);
    vector_v theta(3);
    theta << a, p, 1 - p - a;
    var lp = stan::math::categorical_lpmf(std::vector<int>{2}, theta);
    lp.grad();
    std::cout << "underflowed entry:    lp = " << lp.val()
              << "  d lp / d s = " << s.adj() << "  (exact: "
              << 1 - p.val() << ")\n";
    stan::math::recover_memory();
  }
  return 0;
}
```

In a Stan program the zero typically comes from underflow. In the model below the gradient with respect to `s` is NaN. With `2 ~ categorical(theta);` in place of the array statement it is finite.

```stan
parameters { real s; }
transformed parameters {
  vector[3] theta = softmax([s - 1000, s, 0]');  // theta[1] underflows to 0
}
model {
  s ~ normal(0, 1);
  array[1] int y = {2};
  y ~ categorical(theta);
}
```

## Expected Output

Current output of the C++ example on `develop` at 3d16e8f14e (Linux x86_64, GCC 15.2, `-O3`):

```
categorical, array:   lp = -1.93794  grad =    -nan 3.33333     2.5
categorical, scalars: lp = -1.93794  grad =       0 3.33333     2.5
multinomial:          lp = -1.46794  grad =    -nan 3.33333     7.5
underflowed entry:    lp = -0.554355  d lp / d s = -nan  (exact: 0.425557)
```

Expected: `0` in place of `-nan` in the first and third lines, and `0.425557` in the last line.

## Possible fix

One commit on top of `develop` at 3d16e8f14e: [nsiccha/math@79430e93e7](https://github.com/nsiccha/math/commit/79430e93e74198cbe75fd234d240f71daa87f394).

- The array overload of `categorical_lpmf` counts the outcomes per category and builds one partials-propagator node, with value `sum_k c_k log(theta_k)` and partials `c_k / theta_k` over the observed categories only. That is one node in place of one `log` node per category and a sum over the outcomes.
- `multinomial_lpmf` skips zero counts.

Values are unchanged in both functions, and an observed category with probability zero still gives `-inf`. The commit adds four regression tests: a zero gradient at an unobserved zero entry, agreement with the scalar overload when the zero entry depends on a parameter, agreement with the sum of scalar calls, and the multinomial case. They fail on `develop` and pass with the change. With it the example prints `0`, `0`, `0` and `0.425557`, and all 43 tests in the prim, rev, fwd and mix `categorical`, `multinomial` and `dirichlet_multinomial` test files pass.

The commit does not change `lmultiply`: a direct `lmultiply(0, theta)` still has a NaN partial at `theta == 0`.

#### Current Version:
v5.4.0 (`develop` at 3d16e8f14e)
